(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-11-09"] = {"15.1":{"s":"Alkanes are saturated hydrocarbons with only single bonds, general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂. They are made industrially from crude oil and natural gas, and in the laboratory by hydrogenation of alkenes or alkynes, reduction of alkyl halides, the Wurtz reaction and Grignard reagents. They are fairly unreactive. They burn completely to carbon dioxide and water, crack on heating (pyrolysis), and can be reformed to aromatic compounds. They react with halogens by substitution in light.","f":[{"x":"C_nH_(2n+2) + ((3n + 1)/2)*O_2 -> n*CO_2 + (n + 1)*H_2O + heat","n":"Complete combustion of an alkane; n = number of carbon atoms.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 15.1, p.238"}],"w":["Halogenation of an alkane gives a mixture of all possible isomers and several substitution products.","Wurtz reaction doubles the carbon chain of the alkyl halide; it is best with identical halides."]},"15.2":{"s":"Alkenes have a C=C double bond, general formula CₙH₂ₙ, and are unsaturated, so they undergo addition reactions. They add hydrogen, halogens and hydrogen halides; the addition of HX to an unsymmetrical alkene follows Markovnikov's rule: the negative part goes to the carbon with fewer hydrogens. With peroxides, HBr adds the opposite way (Kharasch effect). Ozonolysis breaks the double bond and gives carbonyl compounds, which locates the position of the bond.","w":["Markovnikov's rule: the hydrogen goes to the carbon that already has more hydrogens.","The peroxide effect works only for HBr, not for HCl or HI."]},"15.3":{"s":"Alkynes have a C≡C triple bond, general formula CₙH₂ₙ₋₂. Ethyne (acetylene) is the simplest. They are made by dehydrohalogenation of dihalides and from calcium carbide. They add hydrogen, halogens and hydrogen halides in two steps, following Markovnikov's rule. Addition of water in the presence of mercury(II) salts gives a carbonyl compound by tautomerism: ethyne gives ethanal, and other alkynes give ketones. Acetylene is used for welding.","w":["Water adds to ethyne to give ethanal, but to every other alkyne it gives a ketone.","Terminal alkynes (with a C≡C–H) are weakly acidic; alkenes are not."]},"15.4":{"s":"Aromatic hydrocarbons, or arenes, contain a benzene ring. Benzene, C₆H₆, is a planar ring with six equal C–C bonds, a resonance hybrid of the two Kekulé structures. It resists addition and prefers substitution, as in nitration, halogenation and Friedel-Crafts reactions. It burns with a sooty flame and does not decolourise bromine water or alkaline KMnO₄. Substituents on the ring direct a new group to the ortho and para or to the meta position.","w":["Benzene has all C–C bond lengths equal (139 pm): it is not three single and three double bonds.","Benzene is carcinogenic and toxic; handle it only with care."]}};
